<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Maharero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Maharero was paramount chief of the Herero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a> in 1890. Faced with land loss, debt and abuses by settlers, he led the Herero rising of January 1904. After defeat at Waterberg he fled with survivors across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omaheke">Omaheke</a> desert to Bechuanaland, while the German campaign of extermination killed most of his people. He died in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-maharero.jpg" alt="Samuel Maharero" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamuelMaharero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel Maharero was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a>, an important Herero warrior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raider. He was baptised in 1869 and attended the local Lutheran schools, where he was seen as a possible future priest. When his father died in 1890 he gained the chieftainship in the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okahandja">Okahandja</a>, but under Herero inheritance customs he received little of his father's wealth and cattle.</p><p>At first he kept fairly good relations with the German colonial administration under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-leutwein">Theodor Leutwein</a>. Attacks by German farmers, economic difficulties, pests and the use of Herero land for railways strained them. Settlers and officials treated the Herero as cheap labour for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and other export crops. Maharero secretly planned a revolt with the other chiefs, and in a letter to the Nama chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-witbooi">Hendrik Witbooi</a> he sought an alliance, writing &quot;Let us die fighting!&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The rising began on 12 January 1904. Its first attacks killed 123 people, mostly German landowners; Maharero had ordered his forces to spare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>, the English, missionaries and other whites who were not German, and he let missionaries with a small number of German women and children pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okahandja">Okahandja</a>. Herero raiders destroyed the Waldau and Waterberg post offices, killed the garrison of the Waterberg military station, besieged Gobabis on 16 January and ambushed a German company near Otjiwarongo.</p><p>Leutwein was then replaced as military commander by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothar-von-trotha">Lothar von Trotha</a>, who brought 15,000 troops and put a bounty of 5,000 marks on Maharero. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterberg">Battle of Waterberg</a> on 11 August 1904 the Herero were defeated by colonial forces with breech-loading artillery and 14 Maxim machine guns. The survivors, women, children and the elderly among them, were driven into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omaheke">Omaheke</a> desert, where tens of thousands died of thirst, starvation or disease; those who tried to surrender were shot. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> countermanded the extermination order, captured survivors were sent to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> on Shark Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maharero led around 1,000 of his people into the British Bechuanaland Protectorate, remained leader of the exiled Herero and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Sekgathôlê a Letsholathêbê, a chief in northern Bechuanaland. He died there in March 1923. On 23 August 1923 his body was brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okahandja">Okahandja</a> and reburied beside his ancestors, an occasion the Herero still mark on Herero Day. He is one of the nine national heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> named at the inauguration of Heroes' Acre near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a> in 2002, where a granite tombstone bears his name and portrait.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Maharero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rhenish missionaries were active, and in 1885 Maharero signed a protection treaty with Germany; <b>Samuel Maharero</b> succeeded him in 1890.</blockquote><a href="/state/herero-paramountcy">Herero Paramountcy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in the Bechuanaland Protectorate. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
